{"id":"d8f2ac3d-5641-4af2-8089-654f89d699c6","object":"chat.completion","created":1784812672,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if 'payload' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing payload')\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    if 'items' not in payload or 'required_failure_items' not in payload:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing items or required_failure_items')\\n    items = payload['items']\\n    required = payload['required_failure_items']\\n    if not isinstance(items, list) or not isinstance(required, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Items and required_failure_items must be lists')\\n    if not all(isinstance(x, str) for x in items) or not all(isinstance(x, str) for x in required):\\n        raise ValueError('Items and required_failure_items must be lists of strings')\\n    # Work on a copy\\n    current = list(items)\\n    g = 2\\n    attempts = 0\\n    while g <= len(current):\\n        removed = False\\n        for i in range(len(current) - g + 1):\\n            attempts += 1\\n            candidate = current[:i] + current[i+g:]\\n            if all(req in candidate for req in required):\\n                current = candidate\\n                removed = True\\n                break\\n        if removed:\\n            g += 1\\n        else:\\n            g *= 2\\n    # Check 1-minimal\\n    is_one_minimal = True\\n    for i in range(len(current)):\\n        if all(req in current[:i] + current[i+1:] for req in required):\\n            is_one_minimal = False\\n            break\\n    return {\\n        'reduced_items': current,\\n        'attempt_count': attempts,\\n        'is_one_minimal': is_one_minimal\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to generate a JSON with one key \"implementation\" whose value is Python source code defining `solve(case)`. The problem: Implement chunk_reduction_schedule. The description: start chunk removal at granularity 2, try chunks left to right, accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity. Return reduced items, attempt count, and explicit one-minimal check.\n\nFrom fixture, case has \"items\" (list of strings) and \"required_failure_items\". We need to reduce items by removing chunks while ensuring that after removal, the required_failure_items are still present in the remaining items. The algorithm: start granularity = 2. While granularity <= len(items)? Actually \"start at granularity two\", \"double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity\". So we try chunks of size g. For each starting index i from 0 to len(items)-g, consider removing items[i:i+g]. If after removal, the set of required_failure_items is still a subset of the remaining items (i.e., all required items are present), then we accept the removal, update items, and restart with \"slightly coarser granularity\"? The description: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That means after a successful removal, we increase granularity? Wait: \"start chunk removal at granularity two.\" Then \"Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" So after a removal, we set granularity to something slightly coarser, maybe +1? Then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" So when no removal succeeds at current granularity, we double it. And stop when granularity becomes 1? \"stop at singleton granularity\" means granularity=1? But start at 2, after doubling becomes 4,8,... until > len(items) maybe. Or if we double after no success, we eventually get granularity that is too large, then we stop? But also \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" suggests that after a removal, we increase granularity by a small amount (maybe +1) rather than doubling. So overall algorithm: \n- Start with items list.\n- g = 2\n- While g <= len(items):\n   - For each i from 0 to len(items)-g:\n        - candidate = items[:i] + items[i+g:]\n        - if all req in candidate:\n            - accept: items = candidate\n            - g = g + 1 (slightly coarser)\n            - break out of for loop and restart while with new items and new g\n   - If no removal accepted for this g:\n        - g = g * 2\n- Continue until g > len(items) (or maybe until g becomes 1? but start at 2, so doubling will become > len and then stop).\nBut \"stop at singleton granularity\" suggests we stop when granularity reaches 1? That would never happen if we double. Possibly the \"slightly coarser\" means we increase by 1, and when no removal succeeds we double. So we might eventually get g=1? Let's interpret: start g=2. After a removal, g=3. Then try g=3. If removal, g=4. Etc. If no removal at g, double: g=6, then 12,... So g never becomes 1. But \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean when the remaining items length is 1? That doesn't match. Alternatively, \"singleton granularity\" might refer to granularity = 1 (i.e., removing single items). The description says \"start chunk removal at granularity two.\" So we don't start at 1. Then we double when no removal succeeds. But if we double from 2 to 4, we skip 3. The \"slightly coarser\" may be +1 instead of doubling for successful removals. So the overall granularity progression: after a successful removal, g increases by 1; after a failed pass, g doubles. This is reminiscent of a binary search but not exactly.\n\nWe need to output a Python function that does this. Return a dict with \"reduced_items\", \"attempt_count\", \"is_one_minimal\". \"attempt_count\" is number of removal attempts? Or number of passes? The description: \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" \"attempt count\" likely number of chunks tried (removal attempts) including those that failed? Probably count of times we checked a chunk. Also \"is_one_minimal\" should be True if no further removal of a single item (granularity 1) would preserve required items? But we stop at singleton granularity? The algorithm stops when g becomes > len(items). But we need to check one-minimal? \"explicit one-minimal check\" suggests we should verify that after reduction, removing any single element would remove a required item (i.e., the set is minimal with respect to single-element removal). That's a common concept in delta debugging. So after we get reduced items, we should check that for each item, if we remove it, required_failure_items are no longer all present. But note: The required items are fixed. So we can check that after reduction, for each index i, removing items[i] causes missing required. That means the reduced set is 1-minimal.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate input: items must be list of strings, required_failure_items must be list. Raise ValueError if missing or invalid.\n2. Make a mutable copy of items (do not mutate original).\n3. Define a helper to check if required items are subset of a candidate list.\n4. g = 2\n5. attempt_count = 0\n6. While g <= len(items):\n   - removed_in_this_pass = False\n   - for i in range(0, len(items)-g+1):\n        attempt_count += 1\n        candidate = items[:i] + items[i+g:]\n        if all(req in candidate for req in required_failure_items):\n            items = candidate\n            removed_in_this_pass = True\n            break\n   - if removed_in_this_pass:\n        g += 1\n   - else:\n        g *= 2\n7. After loop, we have reduced items.\n8. Check one-minimal: for each index i in range(len(items)):\n        if all(req in items[:i] + items[i+1:] for req in required_failure_items):\n            is_one_minimal = False\n            break\n   else: is_one_minimal = True\n9. Return dict with \"reduced_items\": items, \"attempt_count\": attempt_count, \"is_one_minimal\": is_one_minimal.\n\nEdge cases: If required_failure_items is empty? Probably always non-empty per fixture. But handle.\n\nWe need to ensure we only use Python standard library, no external calls.\n\nAlso note: The problem mentions \"chunk-based reduction and granularity-refinement passes\", \"compiler execution is supplied by fixtures.\" But we don't have any external compiler; we just check subset condition. So it's fine.\n\nWe must output exactly JSON with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source code string, properly escaped.\n\nWe need to encode the Python source as a JSON string. So we write the function, then put it in a JSON string with escaped newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's produce code.\n\nWe'll include docstring maybe, but not necessary.\n\nWe'll define solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nMake sure to use only \"case\" key and access case[\"payload\"] etc.\n\nRemember to raise ValueError if missing fields.\n\nNow write the JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":929,"completion_tokens":2052,"total_tokens":2981,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":512},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1653},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":512,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":417},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}